      "isDeleted": false,
      "boundElements": null,
      "updated": 1787326839468,
      "link": null,
      "locked": false,
      "text": "1) How would you make log() non-blocking",
      "fontSize": 20,
      "fontFamily": 5,
      "textAlign": "left",
      "verticalAlign": "top",
      "containerId": null,
      "originalText": "1) How would you make log() non-blocking",
      "autoResize": true,
      "lineHeight": 1.25
    },
    {
      "id": "de_At2-Mgq3kUpOZ04t22",
      "type": "text",
      "x": 1143,
      "y": 191.375,
      "width": 1023.8027343749998,
      "height": 525,
      "angle": 0,
      "strokeColor": "#1e1e1e",
      "backgroundColor": "transparent",
      "fillStyle": "solid",
      "strokeWidth": 2,
      "strokeStyle": "solid",
      "roughness": 1,
      "opacity": 100,
      "groupIds": [],
      "frameId": null,
      "index": "b5M",
      "roundness": null,
      "seed": 1349395610,
      "version": 943,
      "versionNonce": 1637206874,
      "isDeleted": false,
      "boundElements": null,
      "updated": 1787327178554,
      "link": null,
      "locked": false,
      "text": "- put a bounded blocking queue in front of each destination's sink. log() enqueues the record and\nreturns immediately, and a dedicated worker thread per destination drains the queue and does the\nactual write. Concurrent producers, single consumer per resource, which means the consumer side doesn't\neven need a lock anymore.\"\n\n- Worker lifecycle: each destination no owns a thread that runs for the life of application. you have to\nsignal the worker to stop, drain what in queue and wait for it to finish before process exists. Skip it\nand we loose every record in buffer\n\n- Overflow policy: bounded queue forces a decision about what happen when it fills up. Few options:\nblock producer, drop new record vs oldest record, throw exception. Most production loggers default to\ndrop newest with stderr diagnostic.\n\n- Debuggability: write happens on different thread than call site. Stack trace at the moment of I/O\nfailure no longer points back to code that emitted the record. \n\n\"async writes\" and \"thread-safe writes\" solve different problems. The lock is about correctness (no\ninterleaved bytes on the wire). The queue is about coordination (don't block the caller). The two\ncompose cleanly. A single-consumer queue per destination actually lets you drop the lock in this exact\ndesign, since only the worker thread ever touches the sink. You'd add it back the moment two\ndestinations share an underlying stream, but for the common case the queue is enough on its own.",
      "fontSize": 20,
      "fontFamily": 5,
      "textAlign": "left",
      "verticalAlign": "top",
      "containerId": null,
      "originalText": "- put a bounded blocking queue in front of each destination's sink. log() enqueues the record and returns immediately, and a dedicated worker thread per destination drains the queue and does the actual write. Concurrent producers, single consumer per resource, which means the consumer side doesn't even need a lock anymore.\"\n\n- Worker lifecycle: each destination no owns a thread that runs for the life of application. you have to signal the worker to stop, drain what in queue and wait for it to finish before process exists. Skip it and we loose every record in buffer\n\n- Overflow policy: bounded queue forces a decision about what happen when it fills up. Few options: block producer, drop new record vs oldest record, throw exception. Most production loggers default to drop newest with stderr diagnostic.\n\n- Debuggability: write happens on different thread than call site. Stack trace at the moment of I/O failure no longer points back to code that emitted the record. \n\n\"async writes\" and \"thread-safe writes\" solve different problems. The lock is about correctness (no interleaved bytes on the wire). The queue is about coordination (don't block the caller). The two compose cleanly. A single-consumer queue per destination actually lets you drop the lock in this exact design, since only the worker thread ever touches the sink. You'd add it back the moment two destinations share an underlying stream, but for the common case the queue is enough on its own.",
      "autoResize": false,
      "lineHeight": 1.25
    },
