      "isDeleted": false,
      "boundElements": [],
      "updated": 1787328542939,
      "link": null,
      "locked": false,
      "text": "4) Alert on stream that can lag",
      "fontSize": 20,
      "fontFamily": 5,
      "textAlign": "left",
      "verticalAlign": "top",
      "containerId": null,
      "originalText": "4) Alert on stream that can lag",
      "autoResize": true,
      "lineHeight": 1.25
    },
    {
      "id": "qTtxABMdtqh677QcdCjuG",
      "type": "text",
      "x": 344,
      "y": 2847.62109375,
      "width": 1030.31494140625,
      "height": 375,
      "angle": 0,
      "strokeColor": "#1e1e1e",
      "backgroundColor": "transparent",
      "fillStyle": "solid",
      "strokeWidth": 2,
      "strokeStyle": "solid",
      "roughness": 1,
      "opacity": 100,
      "groupIds": [],
      "frameId": null,
      "index": "b5h",
      "roundness": null,
      "seed": 997298970,
      "version": 169,
      "versionNonce": 1180545478,
      "isDeleted": false,
      "boundElements": null,
      "updated": 1787328600750,
      "link": null,
      "locked": false,
      "text": "- Evaluate alerts on the streaming path, not on the stored data: Reading from the buffer directly keeps\nalert latency at seconds even when the indexing pipeline is minutes behind, which is the state the system\nwill be in during exactly the incident the alert exists to catch.\n\n- Absence of data is itself an alert condition: A service that stops reporting looks identical to a\nhealthy service with nothing to say, and the difference matters enormously. Alert rules need a \"no data\nreceived\" case rather than only thresholds on values.\n\n- Deduplicate and group before notifying: One bad deploy across five hundred hosts produces five\nhundred firing rules, and paging someone five hundred times is the same as paging them zero times. Group\nby the alert rule and the smallest common dimension, and send one notification with a count.\n\n- State the pipeline's own health as a first-class metric: Consumer lag on the buffer, ingest rejection\nrate, and indexing latency should be monitored by something outside this system, since an observability\nplatform that goes down silently takes every other team's visibility with it.",
      "fontSize": 20,
      "fontFamily": 5,
      "textAlign": "left",
      "verticalAlign": "top",
      "containerId": null,
      "originalText": "- Evaluate alerts on the streaming path, not on the stored data: Reading from the buffer directly keeps alert latency at seconds even when the indexing pipeline is minutes behind, which is the state the system will be in during exactly the incident the alert exists to catch.\n\n- Absence of data is itself an alert condition: A service that stops reporting looks identical to a healthy service with nothing to say, and the difference matters enormously. Alert rules need a \"no data received\" case rather than only thresholds on values.\n\n- Deduplicate and group before notifying: One bad deploy across five hundred hosts produces five hundred firing rules, and paging someone five hundred times is the same as paging them zero times. Group by the alert rule and the smallest common dimension, and send one notification with a count.\n\n- State the pipeline's own health as a first-class metric: Consumer lag on the buffer, ingest rejection rate, and indexing latency should be monitored by something outside this system, since an observability platform that goes down silently takes every other team's visibility with it.",
      "autoResize": false,
      "lineHeight": 1.25
    },
